body: "## Observed defect and immutable source\nAt main `cd9435e9ca3a83e898af8ee99ee4e17a5dc6f758`, the inline [release merge wait](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/blob/cd9435e9ca3a83e898af8ee99ee4e17a5dc6f758/.github/workflows/release.yml#L386-L498) initializes `awaiting_approval=0` before the loop (line 386), sets it to 1 after any approval-pending observation (line 488), and never clears it. The timeout conditional (line 493) therefore reports historical approval state as current.\n\n## Hosted evidence\nThe [release job 126121084927](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/actions/runs/41624345351/job/126121084927) reports successful automatic approval of runs 41713352866,41713352957 and41713352928 at 20:31:54–20:31:55 UTC on 2026-11-03. At 21:01:34 it reports queued required checks, no failing checks, and refused protected merge. At 21:01:35 it instead says the release did not merge because CI is awaiting workflow approval and instructs a maintainer to approve once.\n\nFresh 21:50 UTC readback shows [release PR 83](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/pull/83) open/blocked at `708e98cab57974fc996e4158d942dd16a5452c87`; [run 41713352866](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/actions/runs/41713352866) and [run 41713352928](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/actions/runs/41713352928) queued, and [run 41713352957](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/actions/runs/41713352957) skipped. None of those fresh reads indicates action_required. Queued checks are not green or recovery.\n\n## Bounded reproduction\nAn isolated Bash fixture preserving the workflow's initialization, pending-run assignment and deadline conditional, with no gh/git/network commands, feeds a nonempty pending-run result followed by an empty result. The actual classifier remains `approval-required` although current pending approval is false. The probe exits 0. This validates diagnostic state only, not a replay of the release workflow.\n\n## Expected, actual and impact\nThe workflow's own permission comment (lines 11–14) says a maintainer approval instruction is needed when its token cannot approve, and its timeout branches (lines 492–497) distinguish approval from settling/failed checks. After successful approval and a later verified empty approval state, timeout should report current settling/check state. The retained historical flag misdirects recovery toward an action already completed.\n\nThe protected-merge/publication guard worked: the failed job did not reach publication or tagging. This is a recovery-diagnostic defect, not a publication bypass, a claim of broken Azure sync, or evidence that the complete runtime acceptance gate failed. No merge, approval, rerun, provider call or publication was performed in this review.\n\n## Acceptance and regression criteria\n- Derive timeout approval diagnosis from current successfully fetched state, not whether approval was ever observed.\n- Pending approval → successful automatic approval/queued or running checks → timeout must identify current settling checks rather than demand approval again.\n- Still-pending approval at timeout must retain the actual pending-run link; failed state reads must remain unknown, not be treated as a verified empty result.\n- Cover those transitions and a later successful merge with isolated mocked shell/API responses.\n- Preserve exact-head checks, protected merge authority, approval policy, all runtime/coverage gates and no-publish/no-tag behavior on failure. Do not increase timeouts merely to hide this diagnostic.\n\n## Deduplication and ownership\nAll 83 current ADO all-status issue/PR bodies and all 50 canonical PM records at the immutable default head were checked; all 50 canonical Git blob hashes verified. No ADO issue/canonical owner for this diagnostic was found. The release record [pm-ado-glif](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/blob/cd9435e9ca3a83e898af8ee99ee4e17a5dc6f758/.agents/pm/chores/pm-ado-glif.toon) tracks release approval/publication policy, not this stale timeout state.\n\nThe same defect exists in sibling [pm-linear200](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-linear/issues/200) and was fixed in [pm-github145](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-github/issues/145) / [PR 148](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-github/pull/148). Those records own their repositories' inline release loops; this ADO workflow executes its own Bash loop, not a reusable shared release workflow. Reuse the established regression approach where appropriate, without treating another repository's fix as ADO acceptance.\n\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (2)\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-15T16:56:51Z)\n>\n> Fresh recurrence with exact-head readback for [release PR #112](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/pull/112), head `855c57fb807c467a9964e448ba192def5df93bc0`: [producer job](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/actions/runs/43707956431/job/132793056174) logged successful automatic approval of CI `43734337208`, CodeQL `43734337098` and Dependabot `43734337122` at 16:14:40–42 UTC, then reported at 16:44:35 that CI was awaiting approval and asked for another approval. Fresh exact-head Actions reads show [CI](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/actions/runs/43734337208) and [CodeQL](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/actions/runs/43734337098) queued on attempt 2, Dependabot skipped; none is currently waiting for approval. No publish or tag from that failed attempt. This is new hosted evidence for the existing timeout-diagnostic defect: distinguish currently actionable approval from queueing, and retain the protected merge/publish guard. This is not an Azure adapter runtime result.\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-15T21:27:30Z)\n>\n> **New evidence on #112: preparation reset green required checks without changing release content.** This is separate from the stale approval message already documented above.\n> \n> * Both [the original 2026.11.15 candidate](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/commit/855c57fb807c467a9964e448ba192def5df93bc0) and [its replacement](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/commit/f191b2385d98cc90920f126e1edbf81183b8b5af) have parent `571d4dcd3a2cae6b9267d21cba1cf4a9729d23c8` and identical Git tree `c2aa54a5350f2775128a1a59bc7efcf14f8f4660`. The [old candidate's CI](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/actions/runs/43734337208) had passed all four Node/npm/Bun jobs by 20:10:41 UTC.\n> * [The producer retry](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/actions/runs/43707956431/job/132895308301) pushed the replacement at 20:44:07 UTC. [Replacement CI](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/actions/runs/43759735350) started all four jobs again. The producer timed out at 21:14:32 UTC, with publication and tagging skipped. Fresh readback showed [#112](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/pull/112) open and blocked and all four replacement jobs still running, not waiting for another approval.\n> * [The pinned release-workflow source](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ado/blob/571d4dcd3a2cae6b9267d21cba1cf4a9729d23c8/.github/workflows/release.yml#L304-L363) creates a new commit and force-pushes the release ref. The result here was a new SHA and discarded progress toward exact-head merge eligibility even though the files and base were unchanged.\n> \n> For the recovery test, start with an existing candidate whose version, base, and generated tree match the retry. Verify the retry preserves that candidate's SHA and existing check evidence, still reads current protection state, and neither merges nor publishes until the exact head qualifies. Add negative controls for changed content/base/version and unexpected ref movement: do not reuse old checks or overwrite a newer candidate in those cases. This is hosted and static evidence, not a live workflow replay or a suggestion to bypass branch protection."
